---
import Site from '../layouts/Site.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import ClaimBlock from '../components/ClaimBlock.astro';
import { release, url } from '../lib/publication';
const cwcIndia = release.claims.find(c => c.id === 'C-CWC-INDIA-STORAGE-20260910');
const cwcSouth = release.claims.find(c => c.id === 'C-CWC-SOUTH-STORAGE-20260910');
const cwcIndiaObs = release.observations.find(o => o.claimId === cwcIndia?.id);
const cwcSouthObs = release.observations.find(o => o.claimId === cwcSouth?.id);
const cwcSource = release.sources.find(s => s.id === 'E-CWC-RSMS-20260910');
---
<Site title="Basin context"><PageHeader title="Water crosses borders" eyebrow="Basin context" description="Rainfall, storage and delivery connect places in different ways. A catchment is not a state, a reservoir is not a household tap, and the dates must line up." />
<section class="section"><div class="shell"><div class="section-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">How the system connects</p><h2>From rain to a household</h2></div><p>Conceptual sequence, not a map or claim about a specific basin.</p></div><figure class="figure basin-flow"><div class="path-flow" role="img" aria-label="Conceptual sequence: precipitation falls within catchments, affects river flow and stored water, then passes through allocation and treatment systems before service at a household."><span><b>01</b><br/>Rain over a catchment</span><b aria-hidden="true">→</b><span><b>02</b><br/>River flow and storage</span><b aria-hidden="true">→</b><span><b>03</b><br/>Allocation and treatment</span><b aria-hidden="true">→</b><span><b>04</b><br/>Local service</span></div><figcaption>A low rainfall value does not by itself establish low flow or interrupted supply. Each connection requires its own dated evidence.</figcaption></figure></div></section>
{cwcIndia && cwcSouth && cwcIndiaObs && cwcSouthObs && <section class="section"><div class="shell"><div class="section-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">Monitored storage · 10 September 2026</p><h2>A reservoir snapshot, not a basin map</h2></div><p>The CWC bulletin measures a named monitored cohort. Its southern region differs from IMD’s South Peninsular rainfall region, and the dates differ too.</p></div><div class="atlas-coverage"><div class="atlas-kpi"><p class="eyebrow">178 monitored reservoirs · India</p><strong>{cwcIndiaObs.value?.toFixed(3)} <small>BCM</small></strong><p>Reported live storage on {cwcIndiaObs.periodEnd}. Capacity and limits appear in the sourced finding below.</p></div><div class="atlas-kpi"><p class="eyebrow">50 monitored reservoirs · CWC southern region</p><strong>{cwcSouthObs.value?.toFixed(3)} <small>BCM</small></strong><p>Reported live storage on {cwcSouthObs.periodEnd}. Capacity and limits appear in the sourced finding below.</p></div></div><p class="small muted">These are not all reservoirs, all usable water, basin-specific flows or household supply. <a href={cwcSource ? url(`/sources/${cwcSource.id}/`) : url('/sources/')}>Read the CWC bulletin evidence record ↗</a></p><div class="grid-2"><ClaimBlock claim={cwcIndia} heading="India monitored cohort" /><ClaimBlock claim={cwcSouth} heading="CWC southern monitored cohort" /></div></div></section>}
<section class="section"><div class="shell grid-2"><article><p class="eyebrow">What is approved</p><h2>One layer, one question</h2><p>The reviewed rainfall observations cover India and four broad IMD homogeneous regions through 23 September 2026. A separate 10 September CWC bulletin describes monitored reservoir storage. Neither series identifies catchment inflows, groundwater change or household service.</p><a class="button button-light" href={url('/explore/')}>Inspect approved observations ↗</a></article><div class="empty-panel"><p class="eyebrow">Basin layer status</p><h3>No approved flow map</h3><p>A boundary, observed series and use rights must be reviewed together. Until then, no basin is shaded or labeled as having a particular water condition.</p><a href={url('/sources/india_wris/')}>India-WRIS discovery lead ↗</a></div></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="shell"><div class="section-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">Questions for a basin investigation</p><h2>What would we need to see?</h2></div></div><div class="grid-3"><article class="editorial-card"><h3>The catchment</h3><p>Versioned basin and sub-basin boundaries, upstream areas and transfer routes with reuse rights.</p></article><article class="editorial-card"><h3>Volumes and timing</h3><p>Comparable inflow, release, storage and groundwater series with observation dates, units and measurement definitions.</p></article><article class="editorial-card"><h3>Who receives the water</h3><p>Allocation and service records showing where water is delivered and who can rely on it. This cannot be inferred from a catchment polygon.</p></article></div><p class="small muted">A basin is a useful frame for investigating water, but it does not replace the administrative and household evidence needed to describe impacts.</p></div></section></Site>
